          "derivation": "Middle Persian xwāstan, present xwāh- [BOYHWN-stn' / hw'stn', hw'd- | Man. MP xw'stn, xw'h-] 'to seek, want, desire; to attract, acquire' is a real and common word (MacKenzie, CPD p. 95), and MacKenzie keeps it rigorously apart from xwastan/xwah- 'tread, thresh' of the previous chart. The derivation Nourai draws — from the 'sweet' root — is the classical one: Horn, Grundriss no. 497 (p. 110) puts NP xʳāsten 'wünschen, wollen' with 'ai. √svad-, svādati „sich schmecken lassen, Gefallen finden“; gr. ἥδομαι' and cites 'phlv. xʳāstan „wollen, wünschen“'; Hübschmann, Persische Studien §92 (p. 198), lists 'xʳāham „wünsche“, skr. svad' among the words showing Old Persian d > h; Abolghasemi (FFD 51) formalises it as OIr *xwād-a- > MP xwāh-, *xwās-ta- > MP xwāst. Against it stands the Parthian evidence: Nyberg (Manual II, s.v. xʳāstan) gives 'OIr *hvāz-, MPrth wx'št, pres. wx'z-; MPrs xw'stn, xwh-', and a Parthian present in -z- cannot come from *-d-; accordingly Cheung's EDIV puts Av. xʳasta- 'cooked' under *huad but does not list xwāstan there at all, and Wiktionary gives Proto-Iranian *xwaz- 'to want', 'of uncertain origin. Perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *sweh₂d- “sweet”'. So: an old, respectable, still-possible derivation, not the modern consensus.",
